<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the best ski socks are thin to midweight, made of merino wool or a synthetic blend, tall enough to rise above the top of your boots, and snug with no bunching. Wear one pair only: thick socks and doubled socks usually make feet colder and cause blisters.</p></div>

<h2>Why ski socks matter</h2>
<p>Ski boots are snug by design, so anything between your foot and the liner affects fit, warmth, and comfort. A good ski sock wicks sweat away from your skin, cushions pressure points, and stays smooth without wrinkles. The wrong sock, especially cotton, holds moisture and leaves your feet cold and sore.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Choose a thickness</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ultralight or thin:</strong> the closest fit and most precise feel. Best for well-fitted or snug boots and skiers who want maximum control.</li>
  <li><strong>Lightweight or midweight:</strong> the most common choice, with a bit of cushioning on the shin and foot. Good for most resort skiers.</li>
  <li><strong>Heavyweight:</strong> extra warmth and padding. Useful if your boots are slightly roomy or you ski in very cold conditions, but too thick a sock in a snug boot can cut circulation.</li>
</ul>
<p>Warmth comes mostly from blood flow and dry feet, not sock thickness. If your toes get cold, a thinner sock in a well-fitted boot is often warmer than a thick sock squeezed into it.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Pick a material</h2>
<h3>Merino wool</h3>
<p>Soft, warm when damp, naturally odor resistant, and comfortable for multiple days. Most merino ski socks blend in nylon and elastane for durability and stretch.</p>
<h3>Synthetic</h3>
<p>Polyester, nylon, or polypropylene blends dry quickly and are durable. They can feel less soft than merino and hold odor more, but they're a good choice if wool irritates your skin.</p>
<h3>Avoid cotton</h3>
<p>Cotton absorbs sweat and stays wet, which chills your feet and causes blisters. The same rule applies to your base layers; see <%= link_to "how to choose a base layer for skiing", guide_path("best-base-layer-for-skiing") %>.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Get the fit and height right</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Over the calf:</strong> ski socks should rise above the top of your boot cuff so no seam or skin rubs against the liner.</li>
  <li><strong>Snug, no bunching:</strong> wrinkles create pressure points. Socks sized by shoe size fit better than one-size socks.</li>
  <li><strong>Targeted cushioning:</strong> padding on the shin and around the ankle helps where boots press most.</li>
  <li><strong>Left and right specific:</strong> some socks are shaped for each foot, which can reduce bunching at the toes.</li>
</ul>

<h2>One pair, not two</h2>
<p>Doubling up seems warmer but usually isn't. Two pairs reduce circulation, add friction between layers, and create wrinkles. Wear one good sock and put your boots on carefully, smoothing the sock before buckling.</p>

<h2>Keeping feet warm and comfortable</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Put on fresh, dry socks each ski day, and dry your boot liners overnight.</li>
  <li>Don't tuck your base layer or ski pants into your boots; only the sock goes inside.</li>
  <li>Loosen your top buckles on the lift to let blood flow, then tighten before you ski.</li>
  <li>If one spot hurts every time, it's likely a boot fit issue, not a sock issue. See <%= link_to "how to choose ski boots", guide_path("best-ski-boots") %>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How many to pack</h2>
<p>Ideally one pair per ski day, so you always start dry. At minimum, bring enough to rotate while others dry overnight. Merino socks can usually handle a second day if aired out.</p>
